# History: cpp-httplib-inflight-cap-planning-assumptions

This file preserves the full content of superseded versions of the
`cpp-httplib-inflight-cap-planning-assumptions` skill. Newest entry at top.

## v1.1.0 (2026-06-20)

- Version: 1.1.0
- Date: 2026-06-20
- Changed by: ProjectAgamemnon #273 re-plan after NOGO
- Verification level: unverified (still a planning artifact — never built or run)

### What changed

The v1.0.0 skill flagged "post-routing-fires-on-every-exit-path is the
unverified linchpin" as its #1 open risk. In the re-plan (R1, after R0
received a NOGO for exactly that risk), the design **abandoned the
pre/post-routing split entirely** in favor of a per-handler RAII wrapper.
This amendment folds that resolution and two new lessons into the skill:

1. PRIMARY — the handler-wrapper RAII pivot. Decorate each throttled route
   handler with a closure that constructs an RAII guard at handler entry and
   lets it destruct at handler exit. This binds the per-request resource to
   the handler's own lexical scope, so the destructor runs on normal return,
   early return, and exception — with zero assumptions about the framework's
   routing lifecycle. The guard becomes the PRODUCTION mechanism, not a
   test-only helper. Exempt routes are simply never wrapped (no
   hand-maintained `!exempt && !rejected` exclusion list).
2. SECONDARY — verify call-site COUNTS by fresh grep before asserting them in
   a plan. The prior plan claimed `register_routes` had 3 call sites; a fresh
   grep found 9 invocations across 8 files. Undercounting a mechanical-edit
   surface earns a MAJOR NOGO. Stale in-code comments ("ALL THREE files") are
   themselves drift to fix.
3. TERTIARY — boundary translation + observability framing: translate the
   low-level "no slot" condition into a domain HTTP response (503 +
   Retry-After) at the boundary; make the rejection explicit/observable, not
   a silent drop.

### Why

The previous version was a *checklist of unverified assumptions*; this version
records the *resolution* of the #1 assumption (post-routing lifecycle) via a
design that removes the assumption rather than verifying it, plus the
call-site-count lesson that NOGO'd R0.

